<h4>50. <strong>What are the two ways of passing arguments to VBA functions?</strong></h4>
<p><em><strong>Ans.</strong></em> One can pass the argument to VBA as value or as a reference. Let’s understand the difference with an example-</p>
<p><img src="https://tumfgdyurswklrzsnevv.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/uploads/wp-content/uploads/2023/06/Screenshot-2023-06-20-at-11.29.38-PM.png" alt="arguement in vba"></p>
<p>Now there are two ways of passing arguments-</p>
<p><img src="https://tumfgdyurswklrzsnevv.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/uploads/wp-content/uploads/2023/06/Screenshot-2023-06-20-at-11.30.32-PM.png" alt="passing arguments vba"></p>
<p>You will get 200 as the output both times if you run cells by passing the values as a reference. When arguments are passed by reference, you reference the original value, and the function changes the original value of x.</p>
<p>On the contrary, you pass a copy of the function when you pass the arguments by value, and the original value is unchanged. In the above example, the second box will display 20, as it was the original value.</p>
